        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.722,
            0.292,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.75,
            0.35,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Instead of many small ones, why does a cell not have a single giant mitochondrion? How does this relate to the concept of surface area?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.065,
            0.83,
            0.35,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. If the skin cells start dividing by meiosis instead of mitosis, what do you think will happen to a cut on the skin?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.089,
            0.48,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meiosis"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.387,
            0.111,
            0.89,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meiosis is a type of cell division that produces gametes and occurs only in the cells of reproductive organs. Gametes produced for sexual reproduction create variations and diversity among living organisms. Therefore, children resemble their parents but are not exactly the same. In animals including humans, meiosis occurs only in the cells of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "testes",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of males to produce sperm and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ovaries",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of females to produce eggs. In plants, meiosis occurs in the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "anthers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (male parts) to form pollen grains (that later produce sperm cells) and the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ovaries",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (female parts) to produces egg cells. In meiosis, the parent cell divides twice, one after the other, to form four daughter cells (Fig. 2.19). During the first division, the cells divide into two daughter cells and the number of chromosomes in each daughter cell is reduced to half. The second division is similar to mitosis where each daughter cell divides into two thus, forming four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes. As a result, each gamete has half the number of DNA compared to the parent cell. During fertilisation, when gametes from two individuals combine, the original chromosome number is restored. You will learn about this process in detail in Chapter 11."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.532,
            0.706,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.556,
            0.865,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Scientists have developed methods to grow plant and animal cells outside the body in special conditions. This is called cell culture. In this process, cells are taken from an organism and placed in a nutrient-rich medium that allows them to grow and multiply. To keep the culture safe, the right temperature, acidic or alkaline conditions and moisture under sterile conditions are maintained. Cell culture is crucial for studying how cells work and for the production of biochemicals, food, medicines, vaccines, and more."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.714,
            0.889,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The processes of mitosis and meiosis must occur in a proper and controlled manner. What happens if meiosis and mitosis do not happen properly? If there is any error in these processes, it can lead to various problems in the body of an organism."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.801,
            0.888,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Errors in mitosis lead to uncontrolled cell divisions. This can lead to the formation of tumours and abnormal number of chromosomes in body cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.856,
            0.89,
            0.927
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Errors in meiosis may result in genetic disorders, which may be associated with developmental problems or distinctive physical features. Faulty meiosis may also cause early pregnancy loss or reduced fertility."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
